this is a production of Cornell  University  well good afternoon everyone so my  name's Kyle I'm in the Missouri Club and  I'm going to be continuing our  discussion on disease resistance but I  will be talking about mapping mapping  disease resistance genes in key  curvature species so I'm going to talk  today a little bit about two different  categories I'm going to talk about  resistance to viruses and in particular  the viruses that I'm working with our  cucumber mosaic virus and then three  pody viruses papaya ringspot watermelon  mosaic and zucchini yellow mosaic and  then also I will be talking about our  work in mapping partner mildew  resistance specifically this gene pm0 my  work is being done in Cuba to pepo which  is the species that includes things like  zucchini summer squash some winter  squash pumpkins things like that and  while this species is very important for  all of these market classes that really  aren't a lot of resistant genes to be  found there's no native resistance to  any of these diseases in keek urban  people so because of that breeders have  been forced to go to other species for  virus resistance the most common source  of resistance for those four viruses are  coming from Nigeria and local and menina  these are too old old varieties of  Cucurbita moschata  Cucurbita moschata is like a  butternut-squash tropical pumpkin that  type of thing and they can cross pretty  well with Cucurbita pepo so we've been  in the past it's been used to bring in  those disease resistances but people  also use this variety called Whitacre  which is an interesting variety name for  this man Thomas Whitaker a famous  cucurbit geneticist and it has  resistance that it's been brought into a  cocoon of people species but it's from  three related wild species so it has a  very interesting pedigree and people  have been using this as a bridge so they  don't have to try to cross with all  these crazy wild species and  very rarely people are using teacher  Buddha Ecuador iNSYS which is one of  those related wild species that is much  more difficult to cross with but it does  have resistance so to get started on  this I was looking for any variety that  was listed as having some resistance to  any of these viruses and I got a pretty  good collection of mostly curative  people some masada  and a bunch of different market classes  but unfortunately for our two most  important viruses CMV and PR SP just  aren't that many resistant cultivars  available right now so what I'm doing  moving forward is evaluating these and  trying to pick the best ones to start  developing segregating populations so we  can move forward with that and have more  of a combination there and for the  bottom two WMV 0mv at least we have a  few more varieties there so we can start  doing more mapping with those so I'm  going to just zoom in and talk about  just one of these viruses papaya  ringspot virus this is a devastating  disease causes just terrible fruit  deformation and then the plants are  stunted and they look disgusting and  nobody wants to buy this so you could  eat it but you went to a store nobody's  going to buy something like that so I  focusing on this one because it is  unique in that it's one of the poses  that in the past resistance has been  described by a single recessive gene so  my my thought is that the most likely  candidate gene for this is this CIF 4 E  which is a translation initiation factor  that interacts directly with the five  prime cap of mrna and it's been shown in  other species that mutations in this  gene will result in a recessive  resistance to mostly pody viruses so  that's encouraging and also a few other  classes so here's a little cartoon of  how this works so yeah for you as part  of this whole translation initiation  complex and it binds directly to this  genome associated viral protein that is  acting as the five prime cap for virus  RNA and if there is a mutation in this  protein and it can't bind properly then  this whole complex doesn't form and  translation doesn't happen the virus  can't spread so so moving forward with  that  I'm just looking for any possible  candidate genes from other  transcriptomic works that have been  identified in curative species and I'm  going to start sequencing those genes  looking for any polymorphism that could  be really leading to these mutations  that would inhibit this finding and then  I'm also working to develop more  matching populations and doing more  screening okay so changing gears a  little bit I'm going to talk a little  bit now about  powdery mildew resistance so perhaps  some of you remember Bill Holdsworth  talking about this work so we have  mapped the major powdery mildew  resistance gene that's present in most  of the Popes parts that are available  today we used a an integration mapping  approach where we knew that these  resistance came from one particular wild  species and it happened in one  introgression event so we were able to  find this candidate interval of about  500 KB that all of the resistant  varieties shared and none of the  susceptible varieties had and with  additional fine mapping we got that down  to about 76 Kb and we developed a marker  that was predictive in key Corbett of  people but to keep going with this we  really wanted to figure out what is the  actual gene involved what what causal  polymorphism is happening right now our  marker is just sort of tagging this  introgression so to start I was working  through this analysis again and I redid  what we did using the new mo shadow  genome that's available which is much  more complete than the Pico reference  that we were using initially and found  one strong signal which is confirming  what we found using the other reference  genome so that was very encouraging and  then I zoomed in on that region looking  at about 80 KB and we have about a dozen  or so possible genes in here that have  annotations and there are two in  particular that I really want to focus  in on and that's this NBS lrr gene and  this part of millet resistance protein  that has the RP WH domain so these if  it's going to be any of these this is  where I want to put most of my effort  because this MBS LR gene is a very  common our gene for a lot of different  species and the powdery mildew  resistance protein with the RP WH domain  is very specific to powdery mildew and  it's been shown in few other species but  that is what is causing resistance so to  really get into this for these two  candidate genes we've been doing a lot  of sequencing did some rt-pcr and we  found that they are expressed in both  the resistant and susceptible varieties  so there must be something else going on  it's not just presence/absence so we're  trying to do additional sequencing and  using race to pull out the entire  sequence of these genes from both  resistant and susceptible varieties and  we're looking for polymorphism that  could be leading to this change and once  we are a little bit more confident that  one of these two genes is most likely  our best candidate then we're thinking  about moving forward with either a virus  induced gene silencing technique to  knock out the expression of the gene in  the resistant plants try to bring it  down to a susceptible level or possibly  using any transformation techniques that  could be like an ultimate confirmation  that our chem gene is in fact the causal  gene so with that I'd like to just thank  the members of Missouri lab especially  page reads for all for help with the  powdery mildew work my committee members  and my funding through Q cap then  allowing me to really dig into some of  these different a lot of different  diseases  which are really important to the key  kerbin for community at large so thank  you  [Applause]  so there you know there are even a lot  of work done in tea curb excusing that I  think one that has been shown to work at  least in proof of concept is I think  it's like Apple spherical latent fire or  something like that so that has been  shown at least to work in  proof-of-concept but not a lot of work  has been done using bigs in cucurbits so  that would be something that I wanted to  use that first would have to get the  system working and then go from there  but that sounds kind of fun to me so yes  so that is it's present all throughout  the United States I'm not exactly sure  where it came from but it also can  affect papayas Tussauds  it's that same sort of virus but it  pretty much infects papayas and then  kicker baits and not a whole lot else  it's not like CMV that can infect like a  thousand different things yes  mm-hmm  so that is a good question there's a lot  of redundancies so if the cell has other  like different versions of this it could  still function normally and it might  just be that there's specific yeah F or  E virus interactions that aren't working  but with the cap protein of the mRNA  that's in plant cells that's slightly  different than this viral cap protein so  that interaction might not work with the  virus but it could still work with the  mRNA from the plant cell it's a pretty  common mechanism of resistance to assess  advisors like that this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at cornell edu

